I have what I need. Now let me implement a global plugin that:
- On `permission.ask`, starts a 10s timer. If no reply has come in within 10 seconds (i.e., the hook is still pending), set `output.status = "allow"`.

Important detail: the `permission.ask` hook returns a promise; if I await a 10s delay before returning, the permission dialog would only appear after 10s — bad. Instead I should return immediately after setting a timer, and set `output.status = "allow"` from the timer callback if it hasn't been resolved. Since `output` is mutated in place, mutating it after the hook returns should still work as long as the permission service reads `output.status` at resolution time. That's the documented approach ("mutate `output` in place; return `void`").

But there's a risk: if the user replies before 10s, output.status will already be "allow" or the reply sets status? Actually when the user replies, the server stores the reply separately and the pending promise resolves. Let me be careful: mutating `output.status` after the user already replied could cause a stale allow to be applied... Typically once replied, the permission object is marked resolved and the tool proceeds with the user's answer.

Hmm, but I can't be 100% sure of the runtime semantics. To be safer, I can track replies via the `event` hook: listen for `permission.replied` events and cancel the timer. That way, if the user answers within 10s, we cancel and don't touch output. If not answered in 10s, we set status = "allow".

That's a solid design:
```ts
export default (async () => {
  const pending = new Map<string, NodeJS.Timeout>()  // permissionID -> timer
  const replied = new Set<string>()
  return {
    event: async ({ event }) => {
      if (event.type === "permission.replied") { cancel timer for event.properties.permissionID }
      if (event.type === "permission.updated") { ... }
    },
    "permission.ask": async (input, output) => {
      if (output.status !== "ask") return  // already decided by rules
      setTimeout(() => { output.status = "allow" }, 10_000)
    }
  }
})
```

Also "полный доступ" — the user said "значит даю полный доступ и автоподтверждение" (means I give full access and auto-confirm). So the rule should also set permissions to allow broadly? Hmm. I think the intent is: the timeout auto-confirms permission prompts (which in the TUI, choosing "always" = full access for that pattern). In opencode, permission replies include "always" which grants for the session.

I could make the timeout behave like "always allow" — the permission.ask hook only lets us set status: "allow" | "deny" | "ask", not "always". So we get per-request allow. That's fine and matches "автоподтверждение".

Let me also add to the config a `permission` block so the default is ask (so the 10s timer is the gate), and document it in the global config.

Now: where to put the plugin? Global: `~/.config/opencode/plugin/auto-approve.ts` (auto-discovered). The user's home config dir already has node_modules with @opencode-ai/plugin. Let me check ~/.config/opencode/package.json.

Also — should this be project-scoped or global? The user said "добавь правило по умолчанию" (add a default rule) — global makes it apply everywhere. But the repo is a git repo; adding files to the repo commits them. The user said "каждый раз пуш и комит и тег" about the reader work. Config files in the repo... The project has no .opencode dir. I'll put it globally in ~/.config/opencode/plugin/ so it applies to all sessions (a personal default), which is the right scope for a permission rule. That avoids polluting the repo.

Hmm, but actually there's a consideration: does opencode reload plugins without restart? No — restart needed. I must tell the user to restart. But that would interrupt our 6-hour session... Actually the plugin would only apply to future sessions. That's fine; I should tell them.

Wait — but maybe I should ALSO make it work in a way that doesn't require restart? No, config/plugins load at startup.

Let me check ~/.config/opencode/package.json to see if plugin deps are there, and check an existing example plugin for the shape. Let me look at the example.d.ts or the .opencode dir in home (there's ~/.opencode/ with node_modules — interesting, maybe a different install).

